# How many pages each program of this corpus draws. One basename and
# one count per line.
#
# A run compares the pages both engines drew, so a page neither of them
# drew is not in the comparison and is not missing from either side of
# it. A program that stops emitting partway through reports every page
# it reached as a match and says nothing whatever about the rest, which
# is how a chapter of this corpus once lost its seventeenth page to an
# uncaught limitcheck without the corpus noticing. This is what says
# how many pages there were to reach, so the run can name the ones it
# never got to.
#
# It is held from both ends: a program of this corpus with no count
# here fails the corpus, so one cannot be added without a count, and a
# count naming a program the corpus does not hold fails it too.
#
# The counts are not a record of what this tree draws -- a count taken
# from that makes whatever the tree does today correct by definition,
# and locks in the next defect the moment it appears. Each of these is
# the %%Pages: line the file's own producer wrote, corroborated program
# for program by the pages the reference engine draws. Neither source
# is xpost, and on the count that xpost disagreed with when these were
# taken -- ch8, eight pages of twelve on a narrow build -- the number
# here is twelve.
#
# The naive readings of the same comments are not sources. Counting
# %%Page: lines gives ch1 nineteen, ch8 thirteen and ch10 twenty for
# seventeen, twelve and thirteen pages, because an embedded EPS is
# paginated too, and a %%Pages: of (atend) says nothing at all until
# the trailer.
ch1 17
ch2 14
ch3 9
